Hi InlandNWRoutes.com readers, When the temperatures get over 70°, we all must take measures to protect ourselves and especially our hiking partners from dehydration. BUT, dehydration isn’t only linked to high temperatures. We can get dehydrated at any temperature, or conditions. HYDRATION Hydration for a hike should start a day before your hike. Getting hydrated and staying hydrated are two different things. Start drinking cool water the day before your hike, to get your body properly saturated. Limit your caffeine intake to as little as possible. Zero is best in hot situations. The morning of your hike, start drinking water as soon as you are awake, and don’t stop for the rest of the day. There has always been an argument about how much water we need to drink. But what you will be doing, dictates your need to hydrate.  I KNOW THIS WILL BE HARD TO HEAR, BUT PLEASE HEED MY WARNING! DO NOT DRINK THE POPULAR ENERY DRINKS. They are loaded with caffeine and can cause heart issues in even the fittest of us. For proper hydration, do not allow yourself to feel thirsty. If you mouth dries up, or you sweat too much, you could be in danger. At the first hint of a dry mouth, start drinking enough water to quench your thirst. Then keep drinking water so as not to feel any degree of thirst. Always carry a water purifier like the Sawyer Mini Water Filter. It weights about 5 ounces, and can filter up to 100,000 liters of water, if properly used and cleaned. The Mini sells for about 27$ and can save you so much misery. I carry one or two water bottles at all times. However, if there is a lot of water along your route, you only need to carry one full of water. This way, your pack weight is less at the start. As you get low on your first bottle of water, stop and filter enough water for what ever is in front of you. Planning your trip, includes knowing where water sources are along your way. A method of telling how hydrated or dehydrated you are is very simple. When you have to pee, notice the color of your urine. If it a neon yellow, YOU ARE IN TROUBLE.  STOP AND HYDRATE THOROUGHLY, IMMEDIATELY! Our urine should look clear. If it is not, start drinking or filtering water as soon as possible.  Slightly yellow urine is okay at that moment. But a half an hour later, it may have changed for the worse. Consult this website’s RESOURCES…TRAIL ETIQUETTE AND SKILLS and our HINTS SECTIONS For additional info. But most importantly, after reading any of our resources, do your own research.  If you are new to hiking, or need to learn more, don’t just rely on our website. DO YOUR OWN RESEARCH. Know what you need to make your trip safe and pleasurable. While you are in any of our RESOURCE SECTIONS, please read and understand everything listed. because….the most important things you can take into the mountains is………………………………………………………………                                        knowledge Be safe and enjoy your outings in Nature. Thank You all for reading and using our free, local website. Chic         David InlandNWRoutes.com

